# =============================================================================
#  What belongs in this repository, and what does not
#
#  IN:   the shared panel and server-files profile (files/), the one-command
#        installers (installer/), the Linux port and its Docker packaging
#        (linux-port/), and the documentation. All of it text. Together it is
#        a couple of megabytes.
#
#  OUT:  the game server source, the runtime data and the game client. They are
#        hundreds of megabytes to gigabytes, they are not ours to redistribute,
#        and linux-port/fetch-sources.sh obtains them at install time anyway.
#        See the "Game files" section below and README.md.
#
#  OUT:  anything generated on a specific machine — configs with real
#        passwords in them, logs, per-deployment client files.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Game files: the server files and the client
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Measured sizes in this working tree:
#
#      Client/    1.6 GB   costume.epk 312 MB, zone.epk 263 MB,
#                          monster2.epk 159 MB, sound.epk 88 MB, ...
#      Source/    240 MB   the vendored C++ game server source
#      FreeBSD/   100 MB   source.tar.gz 86 MB
#
#  Four individual files are over GitHub's 100 MB hard limit, so they cannot be
#  pushed at all. Git LFS does not rescue this either: the free tier is 1 GB of
#  storage and 1 GB of bandwidth per month, and this payload is roughly 1.9 GB —
#  a single clone would exhaust a month's quota.
#
#  But size is the second reason. The first is that these are Ymir/Webzen's
#  game assets and a redistributed server-file package. They are not the
#  maintainer's to license or to host. See NOTICE.md.
#
#  You do not lose anything by them being absent: linux-port/fetch-sources.sh
#  obtains the package itself, or uses a copy you already have
#  (--archive / --reference-dir). There is no release archive of this project
#  containing the game, and there never will be one.
#  Anchored with a leading slash so they only match at the repository root.
#  Unanchored, "Client/" also matches linux-port/client/ -- git on Windows and
#  macOS compares paths case-insensitively -- which silently drops our own
#  serverinfo.py template from the commit.
/Client/
/FreeBSD/
/Source/

# Game/server archives and binaries in general, wherever they turn up
*.rar
*.zip
*.7z
*.tar
*.tar.gz
*.tar.bz2
*.tgz
*.epk
*.eix
*.exe
*.dll
*.lib
*.so
*.so.*
*.dylib
*.a
*.o
*.obj

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Stale bundles
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Do not re-introduce a pre-built bundle of files/. It goes out of date the
#  moment anything is edited, and sooner or later somebody installs from the
#  stale copy. Publish the plain files and let git do the bundling.
#  (files.zip in this tree is exactly that, and is already out of date.)
files.zip
m2-suite.tar.gz
m2-suite.zip

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Leftovers from the original server-file distribution
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  LOGIN.txt and TUTORIAL.txt ship with the third-party serverfile package.
#  They carry its factory-default credentials (root/fratello) and its author's
#  contact details. Neither is our secret, and neither is ours to republish —
#  in a public repo they only cause confusion about which credentials matter.
LOGIN.txt
TUTORIAL.txt

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Generated at install time — these contain REAL secrets
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
m2panel.conf
*.conf.local
panel.env
rates.status
downloads.db
client.zip
.metin2-panel
.metin2-dbpass

# Environment files. .env holds the live database password for the Docker
# stack; only the committed .env.example may be tracked.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.sample
.m2launcher.json
.m2launcher-state.json
.m2install.json
support-bundles/
launcher-logs/
backups/
builds/
release/

# Per-deployment overrides
docker-compose.override.yml
docker-compose.*.local.yml

# Keys and certificates — never commit one, not even a test one
*.pem
*.key
*.crt
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa
id_rsa.*
id_ed25519
id_ed25519.*
.ssh/
known_hosts

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Logs and runtime state
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.log
logs/
*.pid
syslog
syserr
core
core.*
*.sql.gz
*.dump

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Python
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
.venv/
venv/
env/
.Python
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
dist/
build/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Editor, OS and tooling noise
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
*.orig
*.bak
*.rej
*.tmp
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini


# A directory literally named "C:" appears when a Windows path is used in a Unix
# context (it happened once already, under files/). It is always an accident —
# delete it, do not commit it. Note this rule only bites on Linux and macOS:
# git-for-Windows cannot match a path containing a colon at all, so on Windows
# the only defence is noticing it. `git status` is where you will see it.
C:/

# Copies the installer places beside docker-compose.yml on a real install.
# In a checkout these two live one and two levels up; compose can only mount
# from beside itself, so testing the fetcher from a checkout means copying them
# here first. Ignored so that copy never becomes a second source of truth.
linux-port/docker/fetch-web-client.sh
linux-port/docker/artifacts.json
